# The intersection of two intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface"): the larger lower bound and the smaller upper bound, and the empty set when they
# cross, exact. An empty operand, stored as (+inf, -inf), gives a lower bound +inf and an upper
# bound -inf, which cross; the whole line gives the other operand. No bound is a NaN, so that
# le(l, h) is false exactly when they cross. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_intersection_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       interval_set zl zu intersection x=xl,xu y=yl,yu
min_precision: 1
source:        the larger lower bound and the smaller upper bound, the empty set if they cross (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalExact.v interval_intersection_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the bounds are selected among the bounds of the operands and the infinities of the empty set; interval_set states what comes out for every pair of canonical intervals

ml = lt(xl, yl)
l  = select(ml, yl, xl)
mu = lt(yu, xu)
h  = select(mu, yu, xu)
ok = le(l, h)
mi = neg(const inf)
zl = select(ok, l, const inf)
zu = select(ok, h, mi)
